WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.517
Now let's use random numbers for a simple simulation.

00:00:03.517 --> 00:00:08.912
Suppose we imagine that each customer has a thirty percent probability of converting.

00:00:08.912 --> 00:00:14.537
The random-number generator creates ten thousand values between zero and one.

00:00:14.537 --> 00:00:18.197
We compare each value with zero point three.

00:00:18.197 --> 00:00:24.347
Values below zero point three become True, representing a simulated conversion.

00:00:24.347 --> 00:00:28.912
The rest become False. Click Run and look at the results.

00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:34.647
Because Boolean True behaves like one and False behaves like zero in this context,

00:00:34.647 --> 00:00:37.487
sum counts the number of conversions.

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:42.587
The mean of the Boolean array gives us the simulated conversion rate.

00:00:42.587 --> 00:00:48.947
With ten thousand trials, the estimated rate should usually be reasonably close to thirty percent,

00:00:48.947 --> 00:00:52.767
although it will not be exactly thirty percent every time.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:56.927
This example shows why NumPy is powerful for simulation.

00:00:56.927 --> 00:01:01.677
We can represent thousands of random outcomes with a few array operations and then summarize the result

00:01:01.677 --> 00:01:04.088
immediately.
